  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Friday...\n\nWesterly flow aloft will transition southwesterly as troughing\napproaches the region today. Scattered showers and a few weak \nembedded thunderstorms are expected to move into the west \n(Livingston through Harlowton) mid day and transition east. There \nis still some uncertainty on how far east these showers will make \nit. Models generally have the low splitting with the northern part\nmoving northeast but are uncertain on the position and exact \ntrack. With this setup, ensembles have come into good agreement \nthat the low will favor areas west of Billings with precipitation.\nThese areas generally have moderate to high chance of at least a \nquarter inch of precipitation Saturday through Monday. Areas east \ngenerally have about a 25-50% chance. There is some uncertainty \nregarding precipitation east of Billings as this pattern will \nfavor a dry slot meaning some locations may get much less \nprecipitation than their neighbors. As far as snowfall goes, the \nbest chance for snow in the higher terrain will be for north \nfacing slopes of the Beartooth/Absarokas and Crazy Mountains. \nCurrently, there is a 60-90% chance for at least 8 inches of snow \nin the highest peaks. The Beartooth/Red Lodge foothills and \nBighorn Mountains will see chances for snow increase late Sunday \nthrough Monday evening. Currently, there is about a 70-90% chance \nfor at least 8 inches of snow in the Bighorns with a 30-50% chance\nfor at least 4 inches of snow for the Beartooth/Red Lodge \nfoothills. Late Monday into Tuesday, ensembles show the southern \nportion of the low moving up through WY with the potential to move\nsome precipitation through southeast MT. There is still some \nspread on amounts, but there is currently a 30-60% chance for at \nleast a quarter inch of precipitation for southeast MT during this\ntime. Ensembles generally keep the region under northwest flow \nwith building heights and potentially unsettled weather for the \nremainder of the period. \n\nHigh temperatures will be in the 60s and 70s today, cooling to the\n40s and 50s Sunday and Monday. Lows will be around freezing for\nmuch of the region Monday through Wednesday mornings, so take care\nto protect temperature sensitive plants. High temperatures will \nrebound into the 60s and 70s for the remainder of the period. TS",
